<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- EyeGuard Find My cross-check (2026-08-31) -- see
     eyeguard/findmy_watcher.py's module docstring for the full reasoning.
     Installed to /Library/LaunchAgents (root-owned, so the monitored user
     runs it but can't edit it -- same pattern as com.eyeguard.monitor.plist),
     runs as the monitored user (no root needed -- only reads a credential
     file in the user's own data directory). LimitLoadToSessionType Aqua,
     matching com.eyeguard.monitor.plist's proven-working setting.

     LABEL RENAMED A THIRD TIME (2026-09-02): findmy -> findmywatcher ->
     findmycheck -> findmy3. Confirmed pattern across all three: a label
     that crash-loops enough times poisons its own registration in
     launchd/Background Task Management on this Mac -- "Bootstrap failed:
     5: Input/output error" on every subsequent attempt, surviving reboot,
     disable/enable cycling, and fresh plist/binary copies. Renaming has
     reliably worked as a clean-slate workaround each time, but only buys
     time if the underlying crash is actually fixed -- findmywatcher was
     poisoned by a real bug (pyicloud requests calls with no timeout,
     could hang forever -- fixed via socket.setdefaulttimeout(30)), and
     findmycheck was ALREADY poisoned again (14+ runs) before a crash-log
     fallback (findmy_watcher_crash.log, since StandardErrorPath was
     staying completely empty through every crash) could even be added and
     deployed to see why.

     Added ThrottleInterval=60 this time -- not present on the first two
     renames. If this crash-loops again, it now respawns at most once a
     minute instead of launchd's default ~10s minimum, buying real time to
     read findmy_watcher_crash.log and react before enough runs accumulate
     to poison this label too. If the crash log stays EMPTY despite a
     crash-loop, that's a different, more serious finding -- something is
     killing the process before Python's own exception handling ever runs.

     StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath point at the user-writable data dir,
     NOT the root-owned code tree -- learned from the pending_uploads.jsonl
     PermissionError incident (2026-08-27) earlier in this same project. -->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.eyeguard.findmy3</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Applications/EyeGuard.app/Contents/Resources/python/bin/python3.12</string>
    <string>-m</string>
    <string>eyeguard.findmy_watcher</string>
    <string>--config</string>
    <string>/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard/config.yaml</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PYTHONPATH</key><string>/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard</string>
  </dict>
  <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key><string>Aqua</string>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key><true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>60</integer>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/jonahdirrim/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard-data/findmy_watcher.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/jonahdirrim/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard-data/findmy_watcher.err.log</string>
</dict></plist>
